      title: "How to Live Given the Certainty of Death",
      author: "Shelly Kagan",
      url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=O8Hu465KL1w",
      added: "2026-09-13T13:34:56Z",
      excerpts: [
        "We have to be careful in our aims…",
        "Some of the most valuable goods in life … don’t come with guarantees of achieving them.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "I Know What You Think of Me",
      author: "Tim Kreider",
      url: "https://archive.nytimes.com/opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2013/06/15/i-know-what-you-think-of-me/",
      added: "2026-09-09T12:29:34Z",
      excerpts: [
        "THE operative fallacy here is that we believe that unconditional love means not seeing anything negative about someone, when it really means pretty much the opposite: loving someone despite their infuriating flaws and essential absurdity.",
        "We don’t give other people credit for the same interior complexity we take for granted in ourselves, the same capacity for holding contradictory feelings in balance, for complexly alloyed affections, for bottomless generosity of heart and petty, capricious malice. We can’t believe that anyone could be unkind to us and still be genuinely fond of us, although we do it all the time.",
        "if we want the rewards of being loved we have to submit to the mortifying ordeal of being known.",
      ],
    },
    {
      title: "Graham Nash on Joni Mitchell and “Our House”",
      author: "Graham Nash",
      url: "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=cGLpE_Dm2V4",
      added: "2026-08-11T09:21:00Z",
      excerpts: [
        "What I like to do is celebrate the ordinary moments in life, and I’ll give you an example. I took Joni Mitchell to breakfast once in Los Angeles. We left the restaurant, we passed an antique store. She looks in the window, she sees a beautiful vase in the window that she wants to buy. She did buy this vase, and so we went back to her house in Laurel Canyon where we were living, and I said, “You know something, I’ll light a fire. Why don’t you put some flowers in that vase?”\n\nAnd all of a sudden, that incredibly ordinary moment that you and I as men have experienced a thousand times—“Okay love, you do that, and I’ll do this”—when Joni was in the garden trying to find flowers for the vase, she wasn’t at her piano, which means I was. And “Our House” was written incredibly quickly.",
        "I personally think that time and our family are all we have. When my father died at 46 and the clouds didn’t stop scurrying across the sky, and the birds didn’t stop singing, I realized: My God, you have to fill your life with the best you can for every second of your life.",
      ],
    },
